work_accident.reformulated_text:
The process involves several entities: the accident, the insured employment, the employer, the employee, the Labour Inspectorate, the accident insurance provider, and the competent directorate. 

An accident is considered a work accident under the following conditions: 
- it occurs in a location, at a time, and with a cause that correlates to the insured employment (for gainful employment) or school/university education (for kindergarten children, schoolchildren, and students);
- it occurs while working from home, on the direct route from the permanent place of residence to work, or on the way to or from a training centre;
- it occurs while taking a child to or collecting them from a childcare facility, daycare facility, external care, or a school, insofar as the person has a supervisory responsibility for the child;
- it occurs while making use of advocacy groups or professional associations;
- it occurs while performing a practical task prescribed as part of the curriculum and/or the study regulations (for kindergarten children, schoolchildren, and students);
- it occurs in certain professional (training) orientations (for kindergarten children, schoolchildren, and students);
- it occurs in an agricultural or forestry establishment, as long as it does not occur directly while performing the insured employment.

The text does not specify what happens to the accident's status if it does not meet these conditions but is reported as a work accident.

When an accident occurs, the employee must inform the employer immediately. The employer must immediately report any work accident that leads to a fatality or serious injury to the Labour Inspectorate, if it has not been reported to the emergency services.

The employer must also report every work accident in which a person with accident insurance has been killed, or injured in such a way that they are unable to work for three days, in full or in part, to the responsible accident insurance provider within five days.

As a self-employed person, accidents involving people you employ and those that involve you must be reported to the accident insurance provider in good time.

Students and schoolchildren should report accidents to the competent directorate. Schools, educational facilities, and universities are obliged to report every work accident through which a person with accident insurance is physically injured or killed within a maximum of five days to the responsible accident insurance provider in triplicate.

In the case of private insurance, the insured party must report an accident in writing immediately in line with the conditions and to avoid the provider from being released from the obligation to perform. If there are fatalities, this must be reported within three days, even if the accident has already been reported. The text does not specify what happens to the accident's status if it is not reported in line with the conditions.
